<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Pallars</h1><p class="st-meta">872 CE – 1011 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A remote Pyrenean county in the Noguera Pallaresa valley that broke away from Frankish Toulouse and split in two in 1011.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallars">Pallars</a>, the mountain valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noguera-pallaresa">Noguera Pallaresa</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Catalonia</a>, was a district of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse</a> until around 872, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-i-of-pallars-and-ribagorza">Ramon I</a> made Pallars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a> a hereditary lordship of his own.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county was a land of herders and small farmers in high valleys, with castles guarding against raids from the Muslim lands to the south and monasteries such as Santa Maria de Gerri. Its counts stayed largely independent of both the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, and ruled with little written administration.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-i-of-pallars-and-ribagorza">Ramon I</a>'s death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a> separated. In 1011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallars">Pallars</a> was divided between two brothers into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-of-pallars-jussa">Pallars Jussà</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-of-pallars-sobira">Pallars Sobirà</a>. Pallars Jussà passed to the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1192, while Pallars Sobirà survived until it was confiscated by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon</a> in 1487.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 872 CE – 1011 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:77.05%;width:2.77%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>872 CE – 1011 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ramon I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Comtat de Pallars</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=942&amp;focus=county-of-pallars" aria-label="County of Pallars on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4707 669 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Pallars lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5027 925L5013 929M5027 925L5023 931M5027 925L5027 925"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5013 929h0M5023 931h0M5027 925h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5027 925h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5027 925h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=942&amp;focus=county-of-pallars">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
